USBMIDI9 0.1
USB-MIDI 1.0 Driver for Mac OS 9

Read Me
=======

USBMIDI9 is a driver that lets Mac OS 9 use USB-MIDI instruments and
interfaces: keyboards, sound modules, and other devices that follow
the USB-MIDI 1.0 standard. When a USB-MIDI device is connected, OMS
applications can use it as a MIDI input (and, in a future version,
output) port.

This version is the first release. It has been tested with one
keyboard on one Macintosh; please read "Compatibility" before
buying anything on the strength of this Read Me.

System Requirements
-------------------

- Mac OS 9.0 or later (Mac OS 9.0.4 recommended)
- A Macintosh with a USB port
- Opcode OMS 2.3.8 (for using USBMIDI9 with OMS applications)
- A USB-MIDI 1.0 instrument or interface

About the Files
---------------

The USBMIDI9 folder contains:

    USBMIDI9                The driver. Copy it to the Extensions
                            folder inside your System Folder.
    USBMIDI9 OMS Driver     The OMS driver. Copy it to the OMS
                            folder inside your System Folder.
    Read Me                 This file.

Installing USBMIDI9
-------------------

1. Make sure the USB-MIDI device is NOT connected to your Macintosh.
2. Copy "USBMIDI9" to the Extensions folder (System Folder:
   Extensions).
3. Copy "USBMIDI9 OMS Driver" to the OMS folder (System Folder:
   OMS Folder).
4. Restart your Macintosh.
5. Connect the USB-MIDI device.

To check that the driver is working, run the USBMIDI9 Probe utility
(if you have it) or open OMS Setup and create a new studio setup;
OMS Setup should find the USBMIDI9 interface.

Using USBMIDI9 with OMS
-----------------------

1. Connect the USB-MIDI device and turn it on.
2. Open OMS Setup and choose New Studio Setup from the File menu.
   OMS searches the ports; the USBMIDI9 interface appears when the
   search is finished.
3. Add the device connected to the USBMIDI9 port, save the studio
   setup, and make it current.
4. Open your OMS application and choose the USBMIDI9 port as the
   MIDI input.

Notes:

- The port is named "USBMIDI9 Port 1"; the name can be changed in
  OMS Setup.
- Devices with more than one MIDI cable (multi-port USB-MIDI
  interfaces) are designed to appear as additional ports; this
  release has been tested with a single-cable keyboard only.

Removing USBMIDI9
-----------------

1. Disconnect the USB-MIDI device.
2. Remove "USBMIDI9" from the Extensions folder and
   "USBMIDI9 OMS Driver" from the OMS folder. (Drag them to the
   Trash.)
3. Restart your Macintosh.

USBMIDI9 keeps no preferences and installs nothing else; removing
the two files is all that is required.

Known Problems
--------------

- Removing or adding a USB device while USBMIDI9 is running can, in
  some cases, freeze the Macintosh. This is being investigated.
  Workaround: connect and disconnect USB-MIDI devices while the Mac
  is off, or before the USBMIDI9 driver loads. (This problem is not
  specific to USB-MIDI devices; it can occur with other USB devices
  as well.)
- MIDI output to the USB device is not yet available. The output
  port appears in OMS, but data sent to it is discarded. A future
  version will add output.

Compatibility
-------------

USBMIDI9 matches any USB interface that follows the USB-MIDI 1.0
standard (interface class 1, subclass 3). It is not restricted to
one manufacturer or model.

Tested with:

- Mac OS 9.x on a Power Mac G4
- M-Audio / Evolution Keystation 49e (USB-MIDI 1.0 keyboard)

The driver has NOT been tested with other computers, other system
software versions, or other USB-MIDI devices. "It works with the
standard" is not the same as "it works with everything"; please
report problems.

Version History
---------------

0.1 (this version)

- First release.
- OMS input support: OMS Setup finds the interface; OMS
  applications receive MIDI from the USB device.
- No MIDI output yet (see Known Problems).

Technical Notes
---------------

- USBMIDI9 is a class driver: it does not use vendor or product
  numbers to decide which devices to claim. Any USB-MIDI 1.0
  interface is matched.
- The OMS driver (USBMIDI9 OMS Driver) is an OMS driver file
  (type "OMdv"). It uses the USBMIDI9 driver for all USB traffic
  and adds no USB code of its own.
- For best results with USB MIDI, Mac OS 8.6 or later is
  recommended (OMS 2.3.8's own USB drivers say the same).

